00:00:10.956 --> 00:00:13.996
All right. Good afternoon, everyone, and welcome to our session.

00:00:13.996 --> 00:00:18.116
AWS Data foundation powers AI driven legal search transformation.

00:00:18.516 --> 00:00:22.876
My name is Farid Baharuddin
and I'm a senior solutions architect with AWS.

00:00:22.876 --> 00:00:27.276
As this is the last session of the day, I propose some light exercise.

00:00:27.396 --> 00:00:29.276
All right. So can I have a quick show of hands?

00:00:29.276 --> 00:00:32.676
How many of you here have heard of SageMaker Unified Studio before?

00:00:34.716 --> 00:00:40.516
Great. And among you, how many of you are currently using
SageMaker Unified Studio in your workloads today?

00:00:41.356 --> 00:00:44.556
Fewer than expected, right? But I hope after this session,

00:00:44.556 --> 00:00:49.916
you'll know more about SageMaker studio
and how you can use it in your workloads, right?

00:00:49.956 --> 00:00:53.236
Today, we're going to walk you through a real world story.

00:00:53.756 --> 00:01:01.516
How the Singapore Academy of Law modernized one of Singapore's
most important legal research platforms Using AWS data

00:01:01.516 --> 00:01:10.236
and AI services with Amazon OpenSearch service to power search
Amazon SageMaker studio for data exploration and governance,

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:15.676
and Amazon Bedrock AgentCore
to operate agents to begin this real world story.

00:01:15.916 --> 00:01:18.556
I would like to invite on stage Mr. Alvaro Vigil.

00:01:18.956 --> 00:01:24.316
Alvaro is the head of AI solutions at Lawnet Technology Services,
part of the Singapore Academy of Law.

00:01:24.876 --> 00:01:31.036
He will bring you through the challenges of legal search
and their impetus to modernize legal search in the AI era.

00:01:31.756 --> 00:01:32.596
Alvaro, please.

00:01:46.116 --> 00:01:50.876
Good afternoon. Let me start by
giving you some context about who we are.

00:01:51.636 --> 00:01:59.876
Singapore Academy of Law, or Salj, is a statutory body whose mission
is to enable future ready and trusted legal Professionals.

00:02:00.316 --> 00:02:04.556
Our broader mission is to position Singapore as a hub of Asia.

00:02:05.396 --> 00:02:11.396
A key part of that mission is our legal
research platform serving Singapore's legal community.

00:02:12.596 --> 00:02:18.276
It provides access to a vast corpus of legal judgments,
legislation and secondary sources.

00:02:18.876 --> 00:02:22.756
Thousands of legal professionals rely
on it every day to do their work.

00:02:25.396 --> 00:02:28.956
Lawyers spend a significant amount of time on legal research.

00:02:28.956 --> 00:02:32.476
But here's a challenge legal research has not changed in decades.

00:02:32.916 --> 00:02:40.356
Lawyers still spend hours manually searching through documents,
cross-reference citations, and piecing together arguments.

00:02:41.356 --> 00:02:43.356
Part of this research involves search.

00:02:43.836 --> 00:02:47.036
A traditional search brings certain constraints.

00:02:47.636 --> 00:02:50.356
First, it's inefficient and time consuming.

00:02:50.716 --> 00:02:56.276
Keyword searches for lawyers to choose between
overly broad results that take time to sift through,

00:02:56.756 --> 00:03:00.196
or narrow searches that risk missing relevant cases.

00:03:01.076 --> 00:03:03.796
Second, Mr. President, due to terminology gaps,

00:03:04.516 --> 00:03:09.996
different judges and time periods uses
various terminology causing keyword searches

00:03:10.396 --> 00:03:15.356
to miss relevant cases unless the lawyer
knows all possible variations.

00:03:16.316 --> 00:03:18.916
Third, high dependency on Boolean expertise.

00:03:19.196 --> 00:03:26.396
Lawyers, especially juniors, may lack Boolean search skills,
leading to float queries and incomplete research results,

00:03:27.276 --> 00:03:30.236
and thus loss of legal nuance and context.

00:03:30.556 --> 00:03:40.236
Keyword searches cannot understand legal reasoning or context,
forcing oversimplification and missing or risking loss of neurons.

00:03:41.476 --> 00:03:50.316
This constraint increased the risk of incomplete, inefficient,
and inaccurate research directly impacting the legal outcomes.

00:03:51.116 --> 00:03:56.036
We asked ourselves, what would it look like
to modernize legal research for the AI era?

00:03:56.676 --> 00:03:59.556
That's a journey we want to share with you today.

00:04:01.876 --> 00:04:09.916
Our journey has three distinct phases, and I think this is a pattern
many of you will recognize in your own AI initiatives.

00:04:10.476 --> 00:04:12.476
Phase one is legal search.

00:04:13.516 --> 00:04:16.196
A hybrid search system with fine tuned models.

00:04:16.196 --> 00:04:21.636
This is already in production
on nie.com and it's what our users interact with today.

00:04:22.396 --> 00:04:25.396
Phase two is data driven improvement.

00:04:25.796 --> 00:04:30.116
Once we have the search system live,
we need to understand how it is being used.

00:04:30.796 --> 00:04:37.236
We built a data foundation using
SageMaker Unified Studio to run analytics, measure quality,

00:04:37.476 --> 00:04:44.796
and iterate on the product and phase three, and this is where
things get really exciting is a legal research agent.

00:04:45.476 --> 00:04:54.116
This is an Agentic AI system built with Bedrock AgentCore that can
orchestrate multiple tools to conduct legal research autonomously.

00:04:55.036 --> 00:05:00.000
This is currently in development.
The key insight here is that each phase builds on the last.

